window.QUIZZES_HAND={"js1":[{"q":"You run a script in Node and get `ReferenceError: document is not defined`. What does that mean?","options":["document must be declared with let","document is a browser API and Node is not a browser","You forgot to import document","Node is installed incorrectly"],"answer":1,"why":"The language is identical everywhere; document, window and localStorage are supplied by the browser, and process, fs and require by Node.","whyWrong":["It is not a variable you declare.","","There is nothing to import (it does not exist in Node at all.)","A broken install fails differently and much earlier."]},{"q":"Which of these is part of the ECMAScript language rather than a host API?","options":["process","fetch","localStorage","Promise"],"answer":3,"why":"Promise is specified by ECMAScript, so it exists in every JavaScript runtime.","whyWrong":["A Node API.","Supplied by the host, it was browser-only until Node 18.","A browser API.",""]},{"q":"Why does `console.log` work in both the browser and Node despite not being part of the language?","options":["It was added to ECMAScript in ES2015","Both hosts happen to provide their own implementation of it","Node polyfills the browser version","It is a reserved word"],"answer":1,"why":"Two separate hosts each chose to provide an object with that name and similar behaviour. It is convention, not specification.","whyWrong":["It has never been part of the language specification.","","Node implements its own; it does not borrow the browser's.","It is an ordinary identifier."]}],"js2":[{"q":"What does `typeof null` return?","options":["\"null\"","\"object\"","It throws","\"undefined\""],"answer":1,"why":"A bug from 1995 that cannot be fixed without breaking the web. Check with `value === null` instead.","whyWrong":["Reasonable, and not what happens.","","typeof never throws.","That is typeof undefined."]},{"q":"Why is `0.1 + 0.2 === 0.3` false?","options":["Binary floating point cannot represent 0.1 exactly","=== compares references for numbers","JavaScript rounds all arithmetic to 15 digits","0.3 is stored as a string"],"answer":0,"why":"Every JavaScript number is a 64-bit float, and C, Java and Python agree on this result. Never use floats for money.","whyWrong":["","Numbers are primitives, compared by value.","There is no such rounding rule.","It is a number literal."]},{"q":"Which check correctly detects an array?","options":["Array.isArray(x)","x.length !== undefined","typeof x === \"array\"","x instanceof Object"],"answer":0,"why":"typeof reports \"object\" for arrays, and instanceof breaks across realms such as iframes.","whyWrong":["","A string has length too.","typeof never returns \"array\".","True for every object, including plain ones."]}],"js3":[{"q":"`const user = { name: 'Ada' }; user.name = 'Grace';`, what happens?","options":["It works: const freezes the binding, not the object","TypeError: assignment to constant variable","It works only in sloppy mode","It silently does nothing"],"answer":0,"why":"const prevents the name being pointed at a different value. Object.freeze is what makes the contents immutable.","whyWrong":["","That is what reassigning `user` itself would throw.","Strict mode does not change this.","The mutation really happens."]},{"q":"Why does `for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) setTimeout(() => console.log(i))` print 3, 3, 3?","options":["The loop runs after the timeouts","setTimeout batches identical callbacks","var is function-scoped, so all three closures share one i","console.log reads the final value of the loop"],"answer":2,"why":"let creates a new binding per iteration, which is why swapping it prints 0, 1, 2.","whyWrong":["The loop finishes first, which is the cause, but the sharing is the mechanism.","No such batching exists.","","It reads the shared variable, which happens to be 3 by then."]},{"q":"Reading a `let` variable before its declaration throws a ReferenceError. What is that region called?","options":["The temporal dead zone","The closure gap","Strict scope","The hoisting window"],"answer":0,"why":"var would give you a silent undefined instead, which lets the bug run on and fail somewhere else.","whyWrong":["","Not a term.","Not a term.","Not a term, hoisting is what puts the declaration there."]}],"js4":[{"q":"What is `1 + '2'`?","options":["3","\"12\"","NaN","A TypeError"],"answer":1,"why":"If either side of + is a string it concatenates. Every other arithmetic operator coerces to number instead, which is why 1 - \"2\" is -1.","whyWrong":["That would require both to be numbers.","","No conversion fails here.","JavaScript coerces rather than throwing."]},{"q":"Which of these is TRUTHY?","options":["\"0\"","\"\"","0","NaN"],"answer":0,"why":"Only eight values are falsy: false, 0, -0, 0n, \"\", null, undefined and NaN. A non-empty string is truthy however it looks.","whyWrong":["","One of the eight.","One of the eight falsy values.","One of the eight."]},{"q":"`config.port || 3000` versus `config.port ?? 3000`, when do they differ?","options":["They never differ","When port is 0 or an empty string","When port is undefined","When port is null"],"answer":1,"why":"|| falls back on any falsy value, so a legitimate port of 0 becomes 3000. ?? falls back only on null and undefined.","whyWrong":["They differ on exactly the falsy-but-not-nullish values.","","Both fall back on undefined.","Both fall back on null."]}],"js5":[{"q":"`let s = 'hello'; s.toUpperCase();`, what is s afterwards?","options":["It throws","\"HELLO\"","\"hello\", strings are immutable and the result was discarded","\"\", the method emptied it"],"answer":2,"why":"Every string method returns a new string. You must assign the result.","whyWrong":["It is a valid call.","Only if you assigned the return value.","","No method empties a string."]},{"q":"What does `Number('')` return?","options":["It throws","undefined","NaN","0"],"answer":3,"why":"An empty form field silently becomes zero, which is why a safe parse checks for an empty string BEFORE converting.","whyWrong":["It does not throw.","Number always returns a number.","That is Number(\"abc\").",""]},{"q":"Why should money never be stored in a JavaScript `number`?","options":["Numbers cannot exceed 2^32","Floating-point error compounds; work in cents as integers","toFixed rounds unpredictably","Currency symbols are lost"],"answer":1,"why":"0.1 + 0.2 is not 0.3, and the error accumulates across many operations.","whyWrong":["The safe integer limit is far higher than that.","","toFixed rounds predictably; the underlying value is the problem.","A symbol is a formatting concern, not the reason."]}],"js6":[{"q":"Why must a `switch` include a `default` branch?","options":["It is required by strict mode","switch is a syntax error without it","Without one, an unexpected value silently returns undefined","default runs before the cases"],"answer":2,"why":"That silent undefined is exactly how a validation function fails open.","whyWrong":["Strict mode does not require it.","default is optional syntactically.","","It runs last, only when nothing matched."]},{"q":"`switch (1)`, does `case \"1\"` match?","options":["No: switch compares with === and never coerces","Only in sloppy mode","Only for single-character strings","Yes: switch coerces like =="],"answer":0,"why":"So normalise casing or type before switching if you need loose matching.","whyWrong":["","Mode does not affect it.","Length is irrelevant.","switch is strict."]},{"q":"What does `'a' && 'b'` evaluate to?","options":["false","\"b\"","true","\"a\""],"answer":1,"why":"&& and || return one of their operands, not a boolean: && gives the last value when all are truthy, || gives the first truthy one.","whyWrong":["Neither operand is falsy.","","They do not coerce their result to a boolean.","That is what 'a' || 'b' gives."]}],"js7":[{"q":"Iterating an array with `for...in`, what type are the keys?","options":["Strings (so i + 1 gives \"01\", not 1)","Numbers","Whatever the element type is","Symbols"],"answer":0,"why":"for...in also walks the prototype chain. Never use it on an array, use for...of.","whyWrong":["","That is the assumption that causes the bug.","It yields keys, not values.","Only string keys are enumerated."]},{"q":"Which loop form supports `break` AND iterates values?","options":["for...in","forEach","map","for...of"],"answer":3,"why":"forEach cannot break because you gave control of the loop to the library; for...in gives you keys.","whyWrong":["It yields keys.","It ignores return values and cannot break.","It builds a new array and cannot break.",""]},{"q":"You need both the index and the value of each array element. What is the idiomatic form?","options":["arr.forEach(v => ...)","for (const i in arr)","for (const v of arr)","for (const [i, v] of arr.entries())"],"answer":3,"why":"entries() yields [index, value] pairs which destructure directly, and the index is a real number.","whyWrong":["forEach's second parameter works, but it cannot break.","Gives string keys and walks the prototype chain.","Gives no index at all.",""]}],"js8":[{"q":"`function welcome(name, greeting = 'Hello')`, when does the default apply?","options":["Only when the argument is undefined","When the argument is null or undefined","When the argument is falsy","Whenever the argument is omitted or empty"],"answer":0,"why":"null, 0 and \"\" are real values, so they are passed through. That is the difference from a hand-rolled || fallback.","whyWrong":["","That would be a ?? fallback.","That would be a || fallback.","An empty string is a value, not an omission."]},{"q":"Why should an arrow function not be used as an object method that needs `this`?","options":["An arrow has no own this; it inherits from where it was defined","this is undefined inside every arrow","Arrows cannot be object properties","Arrows are always asynchronous"],"answer":0,"why":"Use an arrow for callbacks and a regular function for methods.","whyWrong":["","It inherits a this, which is usually the wrong one.","They can be, they just behave differently.","They are not."]},{"q":"What is wrong with `return` on its own line followed by `{ ok: true };`?","options":["A semicolon is inserted after return, so the function returns undefined","It returns a Promise","Nothing (it is fine)","The object literal is parsed as a block and throws"],"answer":0,"why":"Automatic semicolon insertion is correct nearly everywhere and wrong here. Keep the brace on the return line.","whyWrong":["","No promise is involved.","It silently returns undefined.","The unreachable line is a block, but the function has already returned."]}],"js9":[{"q":"What is the mechanical fix for deeply nested conditionals?","options":["Guard clauses: handle the exceptional cases first and return","Combine every condition into one && expression","Replace the ifs with a switch","Extract each branch into its own class"],"answer":0,"why":"The main path then stays at the left margin and reads top to bottom.","whyWrong":["","That produces one unreadable condition and loses the specific error per case.","Only helps when branching on one value.","Overkill, and it moves the nesting rather than removing it."]},{"q":"What is a pure function?","options":["One that never returns undefined","One written as an arrow function","One that has no parameters","Same input gives same output, and it touches nothing outside itself"],"answer":3,"why":"Pure functions need no mocks, no setup and no teardown to test, which is why you concentrate impurity at the edges.","whyWrong":["A pure function may legitimately return undefined.","Syntax is unrelated.","Parameter count is unrelated.",""]},{"q":"A function is named `getUser` and it also creates one when absent. What is the problem?","options":["It should be async","The name lies, and every future reader is misled","It needs more parameters","get functions cannot have side effects in JavaScript"],"answer":1,"why":"Names are the cheapest documentation available, and a wrong one is worse than none.","whyWrong":["Unrelated.","","Unrelated.","Nothing in the language forbids it, the problem is human."]}],"js10":[{"q":"`const o1 = { n: 1 }; const o2 = o1; o2.n = 99;`, what is `o1.n`?","options":["99 (both names refer to the same object)","It throws because o1 is const","1, o2 is a copy","undefined"],"answer":0,"why":"Objects are copied by reference. This explains why a function can 'change my data' and why comparing objects with === is rarely what you want.","whyWrong":["","const protects the binding, not the contents.","Assignment copies the reference, not the object.","The property exists."]},{"q":"`const copy = { ...user }` then `copy.address.city = 'Bath'`. What happened to `user.address.city`?","options":["It is unchanged","It became undefined","It changed too (spread is a shallow copy)","It throws"],"answer":2,"why":"Nested objects are still shared references. structuredClone gives you a real deep copy.","whyWrong":["Only top-level properties are independent.","It holds the new value.","","No error occurs."]},{"q":"Which is the safest way to test whether an object has its own property `name`?","options":["obj.hasOwnProperty('name')","obj.name !== undefined","Object.hasOwn(obj, 'name')","'name' in obj"],"answer":2,"why":"in searches the whole prototype chain, and a property explicitly set to undefined defeats a plain !== undefined check.","whyWrong":["Works, but breaks on objects created with Object.create(null).","A property set to undefined reads the same as an absent one.","","It includes inherited properties."]}],"js11":[{"q":"`[10, 9, 1].sort()` returns what?","options":["[1, 10, 9]","[9, 10, 1]","[1, 9, 10]","[10, 9, 1]"],"answer":0,"why":"sort converts to strings by default, so \"1\" sorts before \"9\". Pass a comparator: (a, b) => a - b.","whyWrong":["","Not a lexicographic ordering either.","That needs a numeric comparator.","sort does change the order."]},{"q":"Why must `reduce` be given an initial value?","options":["It sets the return type, since without one the accumulator inherits the type of the first element","It is required syntax, and omitting it is a parse error rather than a runtime consideration","It makes reduce faster, since the engine can preallocate knowing the accumulator's shape","On an empty array without one it throws, and with mixed types it starts from element zero"],"answer":3,"why":"The empty case is the one that appears in production and not in your test data.","whyWrong":["There are no types to set.","It is optional syntactically, which is the problem.","Performance is unaffected.",""]},{"q":"Which method mutates the array it is called on?","options":["slice","map","sort","filter"],"answer":2,"why":"sort and reverse mutate; toSorted and toReversed are the copying versions. Spread into a new array first if the caller's data matters.","whyWrong":["Returns a new array.","Returns a new array.","","Returns a new array."]}],"js12":[{"q":"`{ ...defaults, ...options }`, which object wins on a conflicting key?","options":["Neither (it throws)","options, because it is spread last","defaults, because it is first","They are merged deeply"],"answer":1,"why":"Per-key override by presence, which is why an explicit port of 0 survives here but would be lost with a || fallback.","whyWrong":["Duplicate keys are legal in a literal.","","Later spreads overwrite earlier ones.","Spread is shallow."]},{"q":"What does `const { password, ...safe } = user` do?","options":["Pulls password out and collects everything else into a new object","Renames password to safe","Deletes password from user","Throws if password is absent"],"answer":0,"why":"The clean way to drop a field without mutating the original, unlike delete, which changes the caller's object.","whyWrong":["","Renaming is `password: p`.","The original is untouched.","An absent key destructures to undefined without error."]},{"q":"Three dots on the LEFT of an assignment or in a parameter list does what?","options":["Nothing; it is only valid on the right","Expands (it is spread)","Both, depending on the type","Collects, it is rest"],"answer":3,"why":"Same syntax, opposite jobs, told apart by position: left collects, right expands.","whyWrong":["It is valid in both places.","That is its behaviour on the right.","Position decides, not type.",""]}],"js13":[{"q":"Why choose a `Map` over a plain object for a lookup table built from user input?","options":["Objects cannot hold more than 1000 keys","Maps are always faster","Maps serialise to JSON automatically","Keys keep their type and cannot collide with Object.prototype names"],"answer":3,"why":"A user-supplied key of \"__proto__\" or \"constructor\" behaves strangely on a plain object.","whyWrong":["There is no such limit.","Not always, it depends on the workload.","A Map stringifies to {}, the opposite.",""]},{"q":"What does `JSON.stringify({ d: new Date(), u: undefined })` produce?","options":["Both become null","The date becomes a string and the undefined key disappears","It throws on the Date","Both survive unchanged"],"answer":1,"why":"Dates, undefined, functions, Map, Set and NaN are all lost or altered, silently. This is why JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(x)) is a bad deep-copy idiom.","whyWrong":["NaN becomes null; undefined is removed entirely.","","Dates serialise fine; bigint is what throws.","Neither survives intact."]},{"q":"What happens when `JSON.parse` receives malformed text?","options":["It throws a SyntaxError","It returns null","It returns undefined","It returns the input unchanged"],"answer":0,"why":"Unlike most of JavaScript, this is an exception rather than a quiet undefined, so anything parsed from a network response or user input needs a try/catch.","whyWrong":["","That is what a safe wrapper returns after catching.","It does not return at all on failure.","No."]}],"js14":[{"q":"How should a stack trace be read?","options":["Top down: the first line is where it broke, each line below is the caller","Bottom up: the last line is where it broke","Alphabetically","Only the last line matters"],"answer":0,"why":"The first line in YOUR code is usually where to look, even when the top frames are inside a library.","whyWrong":["","The bottom is the outermost caller.","Order is call order.","The whole chain is the useful part."]},{"q":"A function's scope chain is decided by...","options":["Whether it is an arrow function","The order files are loaded","Where it is called from","Where it is written in the source"],"answer":3,"why":"That is lexical scoping, and it is why closures work. Contrast with `this`, which IS decided by the call.","whyWrong":["Arrows differ on this, not on the scope chain.","Irrelevant to scope.","That is how `this` is decided, not scope.",""]},{"q":"`RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded` most often means what?","options":["A number exceeded MAX_SAFE_INTEGER","The heap is full","Infinite recursion, a missing or unreachable base case","Your data was genuinely too deep"],"answer":2,"why":"Genuinely deep data does happen, but a logic error is far more common. The stack is finite in every engine.","whyWrong":["That is a precision problem, not a stack one.","That is OutOfMemory, a different error.","","Possible, and the rarer explanation."]}],"js15":[{"q":"What exactly does a closure capture?","options":["The binding, so it sees later changes to the variable","A copy of the value at creation time","Only variables it names explicitly, by value","The whole call stack"],"answer":0,"why":"The var-loop bug proves it: three callbacks kept a reference to one variable and read it when they finally ran.","whyWrong":["","If it copied values, the var loop would print 0, 1, 2.","It captures bindings, not values.","Only the scope chain."]},{"q":"Why does each call to `makeCounter()` produce an independent count?","options":["JavaScript copies the function each time","Counters are stored per module","The count is on the prototype","Every call creates a fresh scope for the returned function to close over"],"answer":3,"why":"That is the mechanism behind almost every practical use of closures.","whyWrong":["The function is not what holds the state.","There is no such per-module store.","It is a local variable, not a property.",""]},{"q":"What is the memory cost of a closure?","options":["It keeps its whole enclosing scope alive, not just the variables it uses","There is none","It doubles the function's size","It prevents the function being garbage collected, ever"],"answer":0,"why":"Usually irrelevant, and occasionally a leak: a handler capturing a large array and registered on a long-lived emitter keeps that array reachable forever.","whyWrong":["","There is a real, if usually small, cost.","Function size is unaffected.","It is collected once nothing references it."]}],"js16":[{"q":"`const g = user.greet; g();` throws. Why?","options":["greet was not declared with function","Methods cannot be assigned to variables","Strict mode forbids detached methods","this is bound by the call, and there was no object left of the dot"],"answer":3,"why":"The most common this bug, and it appears wherever a method is passed as a callback.","whyWrong":["The declaration form is irrelevant.","They can be, the binding is what is lost.","Strict mode makes this undefined rather than the global, which is why it throws loudly.",""]},{"q":"Which rule wins when a function is called with `new` AND was previously bound?","options":["new","bind","Whichever came last in the source","It throws"],"answer":0,"why":"The precedence is new, then explicit binding, then the method call, then the plain call, with arrows outside the system entirely.","whyWrong":["","bind loses to new.","Source order is irrelevant.","It is legal."]},{"q":"Why is an arrow function the wrong choice for an object method?","options":["Arrows cannot return values","It inherits this from where it was defined, not from the object","this is always undefined in an arrow","Arrows cannot appear in object literals"],"answer":1,"why":"At module level that means it captures the module's this, so obj.method() returns undefined rather than the property.","whyWrong":["They can.","","It inherits one, which is usually wrong rather than absent.","They can appear there, they just misbehave."]}],"js17":[{"q":"What are the two mandatory parts of a recursive function?","options":["A loop and a return","try/catch and a depth counter","A memo and an accumulator","A base case, and a recursive case that moves toward it"],"answer":3,"why":"Missing either gives you RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded.","whyWrong":["Recursion replaces the loop.","Useful sometimes, not mandatory.","Optimisations, not requirements.",""]},{"q":"Naive recursive `fib(40)` is slow. What fixes it without rewriting the algorithm?","options":["Converting it to an arrow function","Tail-call optimisation","Memoising, usually with a cache in a closure","Increasing the stack size"],"answer":2,"why":"The problem is recomputing the same values exponentially many times, not the depth.","whyWrong":["Syntax changes nothing.","In the spec, but implemented essentially only by Safari.","","Depth is not the problem here."]},{"q":"When must recursion be converted to an iterative version with an explicit stack?","options":["When the depth is genuinely unbounded, such as untrusted nested JSON","Whenever the input is an array","When the function is asynchronous","Never (engines handle any depth)"],"answer":0,"why":"Engines allow roughly 10,000 frames and the exact number varies, so deep recursion cannot be relied on in JavaScript at all.","whyWrong":["","Array size does not imply depth.","Async is a separate concern.","The stack is finite."]}],"js18":[{"q":"What does `console.log('1'); setTimeout(()=>console.log('2'),0); Promise.resolve().then(()=>console.log('3')); console.log('4');` print?","options":["1, 4, 2, 3","1, 2, 3, 4","1, 3, 4, 2","1, 4, 3, 2"],"answer":3,"why":"Synchronous first, then the microtask queue drains completely, then the next macrotask, even with a delay of 0.","whyWrong":["Microtasks drain before any macrotask.","Timers never run before synchronous code.","3 cannot run before the synchronous 4.",""]},{"q":"JavaScript is single-threaded, so how does a browser stay responsive during a download?","options":["The engine forks a second thread for I/O","The download is chunked into microtasks","Promises run in parallel","The host does the waiting outside your thread and queues a callback"],"answer":3,"why":"Nothing in your code runs in parallel, the waiting happens elsewhere and the event loop delivers the result.","whyWrong":["The engine has one thread; the runtime is what is not single-threaded.","No such chunking.","Promises are a notification mechanism, not concurrency.",""]},{"q":"Why does wrapping a busy loop in a Promise not stop it blocking?","options":["The loop must be in an async function","Promises are synchronous","It does stop blocking","Asynchronous means 'does not occupy the stack while waiting', and CPU work occupies it"],"answer":3,"why":"CPU-heavy work belongs in a worker, not in a promise.","whyWrong":["async changes nothing about CPU work.","Their callbacks are asynchronous; the point is different.","It does not.",""]}],"js19":[{"q":"Why can a callback not `throw` back to its caller?","options":["throw is disabled inside callbacks","Callbacks run in a separate thread","It can, if the caller used try/catch","By the time it runs, the caller's stack frame is gone"],"answer":3,"why":"That single fact is why the error-first convention exists, and why try/catch cannot cross an async boundary.","whyWrong":["throw works (it just goes nowhere useful.)","Same thread, later stack.","The try block has already exited.",""]},{"q":"A function calls back synchronously on a cache hit and asynchronously on a miss. Why is that a problem?","options":["Two different orderings, so a bug appears on only one path","Caches must always be synchronous","It cannot be promisified","It leaks memory"],"answer":0,"why":"Promise callbacks are always asynchronous, which removes the whole class, this hazard is nicknamed Zalgo.","whyWrong":["","They need not be.","It can be.","Not the issue."]},{"q":"When is a callback still the right shape rather than a promise?","options":["For things that happen many times: events, streams, observers","Never in modern code","For anything that might fail","For anything involving I/O"],"answer":0,"why":"A promise represents ONE future value, so it is the wrong shape for a click handler.","whyWrong":["","Every event listener is a callback.","Promises handle failure well.","Promises handle I/O well."]}],"js20":[{"q":"What is the difference between `thenApply`-style `.then` returning a value and returning a promise?","options":["Returning a value skips the next .then","Returning a promise makes the chain wait and unwrap it","Returning a promise nests it two levels deep","There is no difference"],"answer":1,"why":"Forgetting to RETURN inside a then is the classic promise bug: the chain does not wait and the next step gets undefined.","whyWrong":["The value is passed on.","","then flattens automatically (that is thenCompose)'s job in other languages.","The behaviour differs materially."]},{"q":"You need every result and any failure is fatal. Which combinator?","options":["Promise.race","Promise.all","Promise.allSettled","Promise.any"],"answer":1,"why":"all rejects immediately on the first failure, and note the others keep running, because promises cannot be cancelled.","whyWrong":["First to settle either way.","","Never rejects; use it when partial success is useful.","First to fulfil."]},{"q":"What happens to a rejected promise you neither await nor catch, in Node 15 and later?","options":["It throws at the next await","It retries automatically","It is silently ignored","It becomes an unhandled rejection and terminates the process"],"answer":3,"why":"The correct default, a program in an unknown state should stop, but it means a forgotten await is now a crash.","whyWrong":["There is no later await to throw at.","No retry exists.","It was a warning before Node 15.",""]}],"js21":[{"q":"Three independent fetches each take 1 second. Awaiting them on separate lines takes how long?","options":["1 second","2 seconds","It depends on the server","3 seconds, each await waits before the next request starts"],"answer":3,"why":"Promise.all starts all three and waits once. This is the most common async review comment there is.","whyWrong":["That is what Promise.all gives you.","No.","The pattern, not the server, causes it.",""]},{"q":"`ids.forEach(async id => { await save(id); })`, what goes wrong?","options":["Nothing","It saves each id twice","forEach discards the returned promises, so nothing is awaited and errors vanish","It throws a SyntaxError"],"answer":2,"why":"Use for...of to sequence, or Promise.all with map to run concurrently.","whyWrong":["The failures are silent.","It saves once, but does not wait.","","It is valid syntax, which is the problem."]},{"q":"Why is a forgotten `await` on a boolean check especially dangerous?","options":["await is optional for booleans","It throws a TypeError","It returns false always","A Promise object is always truthy, so the check passes unconditionally"],"answer":3,"why":"`if (isAdmin(u))` without await is a security check that never fails, silently.","whyWrong":["It is not optional.","No error is raised.","The opposite, it is always true.",""]}],"js22":[{"q":"How does the engine resolve a property that is not on the object itself?","options":["It follows the prototype link upward until it reaches null","It throws immediately","It returns null","It searches every object in memory"],"answer":0,"why":"Reading searches upward; missing at the end of the chain gives undefined.","whyWrong":["","Reading an absent property does not throw.","undefined, not null.","Only the chain."]},{"q":"`dog.speak = () => 'woof'` when speak came from the prototype. What changed?","options":["It throws","An own property was created on dog; the prototype is untouched","The prototype's speak was overwritten","Every object sharing that prototype now says woof"],"answer":1,"why":"Writing always lands on the object itself, which is why one object cannot corrupt its prototype by assignment.","whyWrong":["It is a normal assignment.","","Reading follows the chain; writing does not.","Only dog changed."]},{"q":"Why should a lookup table built from untrusted JSON use `Object.create(null)` or a Map?","options":["JSON keys must be symbols","A key of __proto__ can otherwise pollute every object in the program","Object.create is required for nesting","Plain objects are slower"],"answer":1,"why":"Prototype pollution is a real vulnerability class, not a theoretical one.","whyWrong":["JSON keys are strings.","","Nesting works either way.","Speed is not the reason."]}],"js23":[{"q":"`class` in JavaScript is...","options":["An alias for Object.create","A compile-time-only construct","Syntax over prototypes (methods still land on the prototype)","A genuinely new object model added in ES2015"],"answer":2,"why":"Which is why typeof a class is \"function\", and why knowing prototypes is what lets you debug class behaviour.","whyWrong":["Related, not an alias.","It exists at runtime.","","Nothing new was added to the object model."]},{"q":"What is genuinely new about `#private` fields, versus the old underscore convention?","options":["They cannot be reassigned","They cannot be reached from outside the class at all","They are faster","They are omitted from JSON"],"answer":1,"why":"The underscore was a request; # is a rule enforced by the language.","whyWrong":["They are mutable within the class.","","Performance is not the point.","A side effect, not the purpose."]},{"q":"A class field written as an arrow (`handler = () => {}`) rather than a method, what does it cost?","options":["It must be static","It cannot access this","Nothing","A new function per instance, and a subclass cannot override it normally"],"answer":3,"why":"Its one real use is a handler you must detach from the object and pass elsewhere without losing this.","whyWrong":["It need not be.","It captures this permanently, which is the point.","There is a real memory and override cost.",""]}],"js24":[{"q":"Why must `super(...)` come before any use of `this` in a subclass constructor?","options":["It is a style convention","Otherwise the prototype chain is reversed","So the subclass can be extended further","The parent constructor is what creates the object"],"answer":3,"why":"Touching this first is a ReferenceError, and omitting super() entirely is the same error.","whyWrong":["It is enforced by the language.","The chain is unaffected.","Unrelated.",""]},{"q":"`class Stack extends Array`, what is the problem?","options":["It is slower than composition, because engines deoptimise element access on subclassed arrays","Arrays cannot be extended, since Array is an exotic object that class syntax refuses to subclass","Stack inherits the entire Array surface, including methods that break stack semantics","Nothing, extending Array is the normal way to build a stack in modern JavaScript"],"answer":2,"why":"Inheritance couples you to everything the parent has, forever. Composition lets you expose a deliberate surface.","whyWrong":["Performance is not the argument.","They can be.","","There is a real design problem."]},{"q":"When is inheritance justified over composition?","options":["Whenever there is shared behaviour to reuse","Whenever two classes have a common field","When the subclass genuinely IS the parent everywhere the parent is accepted","Whenever the hierarchy is already deep"],"answer":2,"why":"That substitution constraint is what makes inheritance safe, and it is violated more often than honoured.","whyWrong":["Shared behaviour alone argues for composition.","A shared field is not an is-a relationship.","","Depth argues against another level."]}],"js25":[{"q":"What makes an object usable with `for...of` and spread?","options":["An entries() method","A [Symbol.iterator] method returning an iterator","A length property","Being an instance of Array"],"answer":1,"why":"Iteration is a protocol, and implementing it makes your own types work with the language constructs.","whyWrong":["Helpful, but not the protocol.","","Array-like, but not iterable.","Not required (Map and Set are not arrays.)"]},{"q":"What is the practical advantage of a generator over a hand-written iterator?","options":["It works on plain objects automatically","Far less machinery, and it is lazy, values are produced on demand","It is faster","It can be awaited"],"answer":1,"why":"Laziness lets you express an infinite or enormous sequence without materialising it.","whyWrong":["You still have to add the method.","","Not generally.","That is an async generator."]},{"q":"Why are plain objects not iterable by default?","options":["It is ambiguous whether you meant keys, values or entries","Objects have no order","They are, since ES2020","It would break JSON"],"answer":0,"why":"So you say which: Object.keys, Object.values or Object.entries.","whyWrong":["","They do have insertion order for string keys.","They are still not.","JSON is unrelated."]}],"js26":[{"q":"Why is `throw new Error('x')` better than `throw 'x'`?","options":["It is required in strict mode","It is faster","A string loses the stack trace, the name and any instanceof check","Strings cannot be thrown"],"answer":2,"why":"Throwing a non-Error is legal and costs you every piece of diagnostic information.","whyWrong":["Both are legal in strict mode.","Performance is irrelevant.","","They can be, that is the trap."]},{"q":"You catch an error and throw a new one. What must you pass to keep the evidence?","options":["The original message, concatenated","e.stack as a string property","Nothing, it is preserved automatically","{ cause: e } as the second Error argument"],"answer":3,"why":"Error.cause keeps the original error AND its stack. Throwing a fresh error without it destroys the only record of what happened.","whyWrong":["The message is not the stack.","Manual and lossy.","It is not.",""]},{"q":"Why must a custom error class set `this.name` explicitly?","options":["It sets the HTTP status","Otherwise the constructor throws","Otherwise instanceof fails","name is inherited, so without it the subclass reports itself as 'Error' in logs"],"answer":3,"why":"One line, and it is the difference between a useful log and a generic one.","whyWrong":["Unrelated.","It does not throw.","instanceof works regardless.",""]}],"js27":[{"q":"What does a `return` inside `finally` do to an in-flight exception?","options":["It discards it silently and returns the value instead","It is a SyntaxError","Nothing, the exception wins","It re-throws after returning"],"answer":0,"why":"Use finally for cleanup only, and put the return after the whole try/catch/finally.","whyWrong":["","It is legal syntax.","The return overrides it.","The exception is gone."]},{"q":"What is wrong with a catch block that logs and continues?","options":["It removes information without recovering, the failure becomes invisible","catch cannot contain console.log","Nothing","Logging is always too slow"],"answer":0,"why":"If you cannot say what the handler does about the failure, let the error travel to somewhere that can.","whyWrong":["","It can.","It is a real anti-pattern.","Speed is not the issue."]},{"q":"Where should errors generally be caught?","options":["Only in main()","At boundaries where a failure has a defined response","In every function that can fail","Never, let everything propagate"],"answer":1,"why":"A request handler returns a 500, a job retries, a CLI exits non-zero. Deep utilities should throw and say why.","whyWrong":["Too coarse for a server.","","That produces handlers that cannot recover.","Something must eventually respond."]}],"js28":[{"q":"Why does a `try`/`catch` around `setTimeout` not catch a throw from inside the callback?","options":["catch only handles synchronous TypeErrors","The callback runs later on a fresh, empty stack (the try block has exited)","It does catch it","setTimeout swallows errors"],"answer":1,"why":"You cannot try/catch across an asynchronous boundary. Promises exist largely to give errors a path back.","whyWrong":["catch handles any throw on its own stack.","","It does not.","It does not swallow anything."]},{"q":"Which of these produces an error that ESCAPES your handling?","options":["await inside try/catch","A synchronous throw inside try","A chain ending in .catch","An async function calling doWork() with no await and no .catch"],"answer":3,"why":"The rejection has nowhere to go, and since Node 15 that terminates the process.","whyWrong":["await delivers the rejection back to your stack.","Same stack as the try.","An explicit handler.",""]},{"q":"What should `process.on('uncaughtException')` do?","options":["Log and exit (the program)'s state is now unknown","Log and continue serving","Retry the failed operation","Restart the event loop"],"answer":0,"why":"Continuing risks corrupting data in ways far worse than a restart.","whyWrong":["","The state is not trustworthy.","There is no context to retry from.","Not a thing you can do."]}],"js29":[{"q":"`Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'city')` means what?","options":["city was undefined","The object is null","city is not a valid property name","The thing you read city FROM was undefined"],"answer":3,"why":"That distinction points at a different line, and misreading it is the single most common wasted hour in JavaScript.","whyWrong":["Reading an undefined property does not throw.","null gives a slightly different message.","Any string is valid.",""]},{"q":"You have no hypothesis at all about a bug. What is the technique?","options":["Rewrite the suspicious function","Bisect, halve the problem space and repeat","Add logs everywhere","Upgrade the dependencies"],"answer":1,"why":"git bisect finds the breaking commit among a thousand in about ten steps, because halving is a base-2 logarithm.","whyWrong":["Changing code without a hypothesis is guessing.","","Scattershot, and it changes timing.","Unrelated to finding the cause."]},{"q":"Which is better than `console.log(obj)` when you need to see full nesting?","options":["console.table(obj)","console.dir(obj, { depth: null })","console.log(`obj: ${obj}`)","console.trace()"],"answer":1,"why":"And never interpolate an object into a template literal, you get \"[object Object]\".","whyWrong":["Best for arrays of objects.","","That is the useless form.","Shows how you got here, not the value."]}],"js30":[{"q":"Why does a breakpoint beat a console.log?","options":["It is faster to type","It works in production","It shows every variable in scope, including ones you did not think to log","It survives a page reload"],"answer":2,"why":"And you can walk the call stack from there to see how execution arrived.","whyWrong":["Marginal.","That is where logging still wins.","","Logs do not either."]},{"q":"You need to inspect one specific record in a loop over ten thousand. Which breakpoint?","options":["A DOM breakpoint","A conditional breakpoint","Pause on exceptions","A logpoint"],"answer":1,"why":"Right-click the line number and give it an expression such as user.id === 4172.","whyWrong":[" For element changes.","","For a throw, not a value.","That logs and continues, useful, but it will not pause on the one you want."]},{"q":"You want to know what code triggered a particular network request. What do you use?","options":["The Console","A DOM breakpoint","An XHR/fetch breakpoint, or the Initiator column in the Network panel","Pause on caught exceptions"],"answer":2,"why":"Both give you the call stack at the moment the request was about to be sent.","whyWrong":["It shows results, not causes.","That watches elements.","","Only helps if something throws."]}],"js31":[{"q":"Which Scope group shows you variables you cannot reach any other way?","options":["Global","Script","Closure","Local"],"answer":2,"why":"It makes closures concrete, you can watch the captured binding change between iterations.","whyWrong":["Available by name.","Available by name.","","Available by name anyway."]},{"q":"A stack trace is fifteen frames of React and lodash before your own code. What fixes that?","options":["Use console.trace instead","Reduce the stack size","Turn off source maps","Add the library scripts to the ignore list (blackboxing)"],"answer":3,"why":"Stepping then steps THROUGH library code instead of into it. It is the highest-value DevTools setting for framework work, and it is off by default.","whyWrong":["Same frames.","Not a setting.","That makes it worse.",""]},{"q":"Breakpoints do not bind and variables are single letters. What is the likely cause?","options":["The source map is missing, unreachable or stale","The code is minified beyond recovery","The file is cached","DevTools is out of date"],"answer":0,"why":"Check the Console for source-map warnings and the sourceMappingURL comment at the end of the bundle.","whyWrong":["","That is what a source map recovers.","A cache would show old code, not single letters.","Rarely the cause."]}],"js32":[{"q":"Why must Preserve log be enabled before tracing a redirect flow?","options":["It records timing","It disables the cache","It doubles the request detail","Each navigation clears the panel, so you would see only the final hop"],"answer":3,"why":"This is the single reason most people find OAuth flows impossible to debug.","whyWrong":["Timing is always recorded.","That is a separate checkbox.","It does not.",""]},{"q":"The Console reports a CORS error. Where is the fix?","options":["In your fetch options","On the server, it must send the CORS headers","In the DNS configuration","In the browser settings"],"answer":1,"why":"The request usually succeeded; the browser refused to let your JavaScript read the response. Changing fetch options changes which rules apply, not whether they are enforced.","whyWrong":["That changes the request, not the permission.","","Unrelated.","Not a user setting."]},{"q":"What is the difference between a 401 and a 403?","options":["401 is for APIs, 403 for pages","401 means no valid credential arrived; 403 means it did and lacks permission","They are interchangeable","403 means the resource does not exist"],"answer":1,"why":"They lead to completely different investigations, which is why collapsing them wastes time.","whyWrong":["Both apply anywhere.","","They mean different things.","That is 404."]}],"js33":[{"q":"A login flow works in incognito but not normally. What should you check first?","options":["The server logs","Your network speed","Stale storage or a stale cookie, clear storage","The JavaScript bundle"],"answer":2,"why":"Five seconds in the Application panel settles the question before you debug anything else.","whyWrong":["Only after ruling out the client state.","Unrelated.","","Unrelated."]},{"q":"`invalid_grant` at the token endpoint most often means what?","options":["The client secret is wrong","The redirect_uri is unregistered","The code was already used, expired, or the code_verifier does not match","The user is not permitted"],"answer":2,"why":"A double-mounted component redeeming the code twice is a common cause, an XHR breakpoint on /token catches it.","whyWrong":["That reports as invalid_client.","That is redirect_uri_mismatch, at the authorize step.","","That is a consent or scope error."]},{"q":"What is wrong with pasting a real id_token into an online decoder?","options":["Online decoders cannot handle real tokens","It invalidates the token","It is a live credential and you have just given it away","Nothing"],"answer":2,"why":"Decode locally in the Console instead, and remember decoding is not verifying.","whyWrong":["They can, which is the problem.","It stays valid, in someone else's hands.","","It is a real risk."]}],"js34":[{"q":"Why can you not write `if (cond) import { a } from './a.js'`?","options":["Imports are static and resolved before any code runs","You can","import is a reserved word in blocks","Only default imports may be conditional"],"answer":0,"why":"Use the dynamic form, await import(), which returns a promise, that is how route-based code splitting works.","whyWrong":["","It is a SyntaxError.","It is the static structure, not the keyword.","Neither form may be."]},{"q":"Why prefer named exports over a default export?","options":["They are faster to load","Named exports allow more per file","Defaults are deprecated","A typo becomes a build error rather than undefined, and the name stays greppable"],"answer":3,"why":"Each importer can rename a default to anything, which quietly makes a symbol impossible to find.","whyWrong":["Load time is unaffected.","Count is not the issue.","They are not.",""]},{"q":"Code that worked under a bundler fails in Node with a module-not-found error. What is the likely cause?","options":["A missing .js extension: Node requires it, bundlers historically did not","A circular import","The package.json is corrupt","Node does not support ESM"],"answer":0,"why":"Browsers require the extension too, so the bundler was papering over it.","whyWrong":["","A cycle fails differently.","Unlikely.","It has for years."]}],"js35":[{"q":"What decides whether a `.js` file is treated as ESM or CommonJS?","options":["The \"type\" field in the nearest package.json","The presence of an import statement","The Node version","The file's first line"],"answer":0,"why":"A .mjs or .cjs extension overrides it unconditionally. No type field means CommonJS, which is why 'Cannot use import statement outside a module' is the error people hit first.","whyWrong":["","Node does not sniff the contents.","Version is unrelated.","There is no pragma."]},{"q":"Why can CommonJS not be tree-shaken?","options":["It can be, provided every export is declared at the top level rather than assigned conditionally","It is too old, so bundlers deliberately skip the optimization rather than risk breaking it","require is a function call that could take any string, so the graph is not statically known","Bundlers refuse to parse it, since the module format predates the tooling that performs the analysis"],"answer":2,"why":"ESM's imports are resolved before execution, which is what lets tools drop unused exports.","whyWrong":["Not reliably.","Age is not the reason.","","They parse it fine."]},{"q":"What should a new Node project use?","options":["CommonJS, for compatibility","Whichever the first dependency uses","Both, published as a dual package","ESM only, unless it must support old consumers"],"answer":3,"why":"Dual-package publishing is one of the genuinely fiddly parts of the ecosystem, avoid it if you can.","whyWrong":["ESM is the standard and works everywhere now.","Your own modules need not follow.","Only if you must.",""]}],"js36":[{"q":"What does `^4.19.2` allow?","options":["Any 4.19.x","Any version at or above 4.19.2","Any 4.x at or above 4.19.2, but not 5.0.0","Exactly 4.19.2"],"answer":2,"why":"The caret never crosses a major, except for 0.x, where it treats the minor as breaking.","whyWrong":["That is the tilde.","It stops before the next major.","","That is a bare version."]},{"q":"What is the difference between `npm install` and `npm ci`?","options":["install is for development, ci for production, otherwise identical","ci is just faster","install resolves ranges and rewrites the lockfile; ci installs exactly what is pinned","There is none"],"answer":2,"why":"Running install in CI is how a build that passed yesterday fails today with a dependency nobody changed.","whyWrong":["The behavioural difference is what matters.","It is faster, but determinism is the point.",""," They differ materially."]},{"q":"Why commit the lockfile even for a library?","options":["It reduces install size","npm refuses to publish without it","It makes YOUR builds reproducible; it does not affect consumers","Consumers need it to install"],"answer":2,"why":"Consumers resolve their own tree, so your lockfile costs them nothing and buys you a reproducible CI.","whyWrong":["No effect.","It does not.","","They resolve independently."]}],"js37":[{"q":"Browsers support modules natively. Why bundle at all?","options":["Fewer requests, tree-shaking, non-JS assets, and bare specifier resolution","Browsers do not really support them","Bundling is required by npm","To minify"],"answer":0,"why":"In development the calculation reverses, which is why Vite serves unbundled ESM.","whyWrong":["","They do.","It is not.","Minification is separate."]},{"q":"What defeats tree-shaking?","options":["Using too many exports","CommonJS dependencies and top-level side effects","Source maps","Arrow functions"],"answer":1,"why":"The sideEffects: false field is how a library promises it has none so the bundler can drop unused modules.","whyWrong":["Unused ones are dropped.","","Irrelevant.","Irrelevant."]},{"q":"What is the difference between a linter and a formatter?","options":["A formatter checks types","A linter only checks style","They are the same tool","A linter finds likely mistakes; a formatter has no opinions worth arguing about"],"answer":3,"why":"Prettier's value is not its output, it is that it ends the discussion. Do not configure it.","whyWrong":["That is a type checker.","Modern linters focus on correctness.","Different jobs.",""]}],"js50":[{"q":"What happens to TypeScript type annotations when the code runs?","options":["They become runtime assertions","They are erased (there are no runtime type checks)","They stay as metadata","They are compiled into validation functions"],"answer":1,"why":"Which is why every boundary still needs a real runtime check.","whyWrong":["No checks are generated.","","Nothing survives.","Nothing is generated."]},{"q":"`const user = await res.json() as User`, what does the `as` do?","options":["Converts the object to a User","Tells the compiler to stop checking; it verifies nothing","Validates the shape at runtime","Throws if the shape is wrong"],"answer":1,"why":"A schema library validates at runtime AND produces the type, with no chance of the two drifting apart.","whyWrong":["No conversion happens.","","It is an assertion, not a check.","It cannot, it does not exist at runtime."]},{"q":"Which tsconfig setting matters most, and why?","options":["strict, because strictNullChecks catches the commonest runtime error in JavaScript","module, for Node compatibility","target, for browser support","noEmit, for speed"],"answer":0,"why":"Adding it to an existing codebase later is genuinely painful, so turn it on from day one.","whyWrong":["","Important, not the most.","Important, not the most.","A build detail."]}],"js51":[{"q":"TypeScript uses structural typing. What does that mean?","options":["If the shape fits, it fits, nothing declares that it implements an interface","Types are checked by name","Extra properties always cause an error","Only classes can satisfy an interface"],"answer":0,"why":"It is what makes TypeScript feel natural over JavaScript, where nobody declares interfaces either.","whyWrong":["","That is nominal typing, as in Java.","Only on object literals assigned directly.","Any object with the right shape works."]},{"q":"When should you reach for a generic?","options":["Whenever a function takes an object","Whenever you would otherwise write any","To make a function reusable","When the output type depends on the input type"],"answer":3,"why":"Writing T everywhere because it looks professional produces signatures nobody can read.","whyWrong":["Not by itself.","unknown is usually the right answer there.","Reuse does not require a type parameter.",""]},{"q":"You need an API response type that is a User without its password field. Which utility type?","options":["Readonly<User>","Partial<User>","Pick<User, 'password'>","Omit<User, 'password'>"],"answer":3,"why":"Pick selects a subset; Omit removes one. Partial makes everything optional, which is for patch payloads.","whyWrong":["Prevents reassignment.","Makes every field optional.","Selects only password, the opposite.",""]}],"js52":[{"q":"Why is `unknown` better than `any` at a boundary?","options":["any is deprecated","There is no difference","unknown is faster to compile","unknown keeps the checking on, you must prove the type before using it"],"answer":3,"why":"any disables checking for everything it touches and spreads through the codebase silently.","whyWrong":["It is not deprecated, just dangerous.","They are opposites.","Compile time is unaffected.",""]},{"q":"Which narrowing check works for distinguishing a Date from a string?","options":["Array.isArray","instanceof","the in operator","typeof"],"answer":1,"why":"typeof a Date is \"object\", the same as every other object, the same limitation as typeof null and typeof [].","whyWrong":["For arrays.","","For distinguishing object shapes by key.","Reports \"object\" for both Dates and plain objects."]},{"q":"What is the right way to add types to an existing JavaScript codebase?","options":["Add any everywhere, then refine","One big branch converting everything","Incrementally: allowJs and checkJs first, then file by file, leaves first","Rewrite in TypeScript from scratch"],"answer":2,"why":"A whole-codebase branch will not merge, and the types you write while fighting a rebase are not the ones you want.","whyWrong":["any spreads and is hard to remove later.","It will not merge.","","Throws away working code."]}],"jscap":[{"q":"Why must a link shortener validate the URL scheme with an allowlist?","options":["new URL rejects them already","To support internationalised domains","Blocklists are slower","javascript: and data: parse as valid URLs, so 'did it parse?' lets them through"],"answer":3,"why":"A stored javascript: URL later rendered as an href turns your shortener into an XSS delivery service.","whyWrong":["It parses them successfully.","Unrelated.","Speed is not the issue.",""]},{"q":"In the store, why must the duplicate-URL check come BEFORE the capacity check?","options":["Capacity checks are expensive","Otherwise a full store refuses to return a code it already has","The Map would throw","Order does not matter"],"answer":1,"why":"A repeat save adds nothing, so capacity is irrelevant to it, and the user sees the bug as 'my link stopped working'.","whyWrong":["Both are trivial.","","It would not.","It changes the behaviour."]},{"q":"Every metric is bad at once: loop lag, error rate, latency and rejections. What do you investigate first?","options":["The error rate, since users see it","The highest number","The rate limiter, since it is cheapest to change","The blocked event loop (it inflates all the others)"],"answer":3,"why":"Diagnosis is an ordered list of causes, not a scoring function. Chasing a symptom first is how a twenty-minute fix becomes a two-day outage.","whyWrong":["Errors here are downstream of the blockage.","Magnitude does not indicate causation.","Changing it would mask the real cause.",""]}],"js38":[{"q":"What is Node.js, precisely?","options":["A browser without a window","A JavaScript dialect","The V8 engine packaged with libraries for files, sockets and processes","A compiler to native code"],"answer":2,"why":"The language is identical; what changes is the surroundings, no DOM, and in its place fs, http, os and process.","whyWrong":["No rendering engine at all.","Same language.","","It interprets and JITs, like the browser."]},{"q":"Why is there no same-origin policy in Node?","options":["A browser sandboxes code it downloaded from a stranger; you chose to run this","It is enabled with a flag","Node predates the policy","There is one"],"answer":0,"why":"Which is exactly why installing an untrusted npm package is a serious act.","whyWrong":["","No such flag.","Age is not the reason.","There is not."]},{"q":"Which Node version should run in production?","options":["An even-numbered LTS release","The newest release, always","An odd-numbered release, for the features","Whatever is on the machine"],"answer":0,"why":"Pin it in three places that must agree: engines in package.json, the CI setup step, and the Docker base image.","whyWrong":["","Odd majors are not supported long-term.","Odd releases are for trying features.","That is how you get a syntax error the server cannot parse."]}],"js39":[{"q":"Which queue does Node drain FIRST between phases?","options":["setImmediate","Promise microtasks","Timers","process.nextTick, before promise microtasks"],"answer":3,"why":"A recursive nextTick starves the loop completely, which is why queueMicrotask is usually the safer choice.","whyWrong":["The check phase.","nextTick jumps ahead of them.","A phase, not a between-phase queue.",""]},{"q":"Why is blocking worse in Node than in a browser?","options":["Node has no JIT","It is not worse","The stack is smaller","One loop serves every connected client, not one user"],"answer":3,"why":"A synchronous 200ms operation on a server handling 100 requests per second is not a slow endpoint, it is an outage.","whyWrong":["It has the same JIT.","It is materially worse.","Same engine.",""]},{"q":"Which of these actually runs off the main thread?","options":["A tight loop over a million rows","Asynchronous file I/O, via the thread pool","JSON.parse of a large string","A regex with catastrophic backtracking"],"answer":1,"why":"Network I/O uses no pool at all, it is genuinely event-driven. CPU work belongs in worker_threads.","whyWrong":["Synchronous.","","Synchronous, and unavoidable, so bound the size.","Synchronous, and a real denial-of-service vector."]}],"js40":[{"q":"`process.env.DEBUG` is the string `\"false\"`. Is it truthy?","options":["No, the string false is falsy","It is a boolean","Yes, every non-empty string is truthy","Only in strict mode"],"answer":2,"why":"This turns feature flags on in production more often than any other mistake in configuration handling.","whyWrong":["Only the eight falsy values are falsy.","Everything in env is a string.","","Mode does not affect coercion."]},{"q":"Where should required environment variables be validated?","options":["At startup, exiting with a clear message if anything is missing","In a try/catch around each read","At the point of first use","In the CI pipeline only"],"answer":0,"why":"Discovering a missing database URL when the first request arrives at 3am is strictly worse than failing to start.","whyWrong":["","It defers the failure.","Too late, and scattered.","CI cannot see production values."]},{"q":"What is the risk of calling `process.exit()` directly?","options":["It skips the exit code","It restarts the process","It cannot be caught","It is immediate, pending async work is abandoned and buffered output may be lost"],"answer":3,"why":"Let the loop drain, and use exit only after cleanup or on a fatal error.","whyWrong":["The code is honoured.","It does not.","True, and not the main risk.",""]}],"js41":[{"q":"When is `readFileSync` acceptable?","options":["Whenever the file is under 1MB","Never","At startup, before the server is listening","Inside a request handler for small files"],"answer":2,"why":"In a hot path it stops every connected client, and it shows up as the whole service being slow rather than one endpoint.","whyWrong":["Size does not change the blocking.","It is fine at startup.","","Never in a handler."]},{"q":"`await fs.readFile('a.txt')` with no encoding returns what?","options":["An error","null","A Buffer of raw bytes","A string"],"answer":2,"why":"String methods on a Buffer behave strangely rather than throwing, which makes it an annoying bug to spot.","whyWrong":["It succeeds.","It succeeds.","","Only with an encoding argument."]},{"q":"Why is `if (await exists(p)) await readFile(p)` a bad pattern?","options":["It is two round trips","It does not handle directories","The file can be deleted between the check and the read (a TOCTOU race)","exists is deprecated"],"answer":2,"why":"Attempt the operation and handle e.code === 'ENOENT' instead.","whyWrong":["True, and not the main issue.","A separate concern.","","Deprecation is a symptom of this problem."]}],"js42":[{"q":"Why is checking a user-supplied path for `\"..\"` not enough to stop path traversal?","options":["It is enough","It is too slow","Encoding, alternate separators and symlinks all bypass a substring check","It also blocks legitimate paths"],"answer":2,"why":"Resolve the path, then verify the result still starts with the base directory plus a separator.","whyWrong":["Blocklists lose.","Speed is irrelevant.","","True as well, and not the security failure."]},{"q":"Why must the containment check compare against the base plus a separator?","options":["To handle Windows paths","Otherwise /uploads-evil passes a startsWith('/uploads') test","To normalise case","startsWith requires a trailing slash"],"answer":1,"why":"And the check needs an exact-match branch too, so the base directory itself is allowed.","whyWrong":["A separate concern.","","A separate concern.","It does not."]},{"q":"What removes the whole class of filename problems rather than filtering it?","options":["Lowercase everything","Escape the filename","Reject non-ASCII names","Generate your own name and store the user's as metadata"],"answer":3,"why":"You never trust a filename, so do not use one as a path.","whyWrong":["Filtering.","Escaping is filtering.","Filtering.",""]}],"js43":[{"q":"What problem does backpressure solve?","options":["Corrupted chunks","Slow disks","A fast reader outpacing a slow writer, filling memory silently","Too many open files"],"answer":2,"why":"Without coordination the unwritten data piles up and you have reinvented loading the whole file, less obviously.","whyWrong":["A separate concern.","Speed is the cause, not the problem.","","A separate concern."]},{"q":"Why use `pipeline` rather than `.pipe()`?","options":["It is faster","It propagates errors and cleans up every stream if any fails","pipe is deprecated","It adds compression"],"answer":1,"why":".pipe leaks handles on error, which is precisely why pipeline exists.","whyWrong":["Throughput is the same.","","It is not formally deprecated.","Only if you add a transform."]},{"q":"Decoding each chunk of a stream separately can corrupt text. Why?","options":["A multi-byte character can be split across two chunks","Buffers are not thread-safe","The encoding changes mid-stream","It cannot"],"answer":0,"why":"setEncoding on the stream, or StringDecoder, hold partial characters back until they are complete.","whyWrong":["","Threads are not involved.","It does not.","It can, and does."]}],"js44":[{"q":"What happens if a request handler never calls `res.end()`?","options":["It throws","The request hangs until the client times out, and the connection stays open","The response is empty but complete","Node sends a 500 automatically"],"answer":1,"why":"Enough of them exhaust the server, which is why every path, including the error branches, must end the response exactly once.","whyWrong":["It hangs silently.","","Nothing is sent.","It does not."]},{"q":"Why must you bound the size of a request body?","options":["Node caps it at 1MB anyway","One client can otherwise send an endless body and exhaust your memory for free","To set content-length","JSON.parse requires it"],"answer":1,"why":"And the check has to run inside the loop, so you stop at the limit rather than after buffering the whole payload.","whyWrong":["There is no default cap.","","A separate concern.","It does not."]},{"q":"`req.url` gives you what?","options":["The Host header","The path only","The full absolute URL","The path and query only, not the scheme or host"],"answer":3,"why":"Parse it with `new URL(req.url, 'http://' + req.headers.host)` rather than splitting on '?'.","whyWrong":["Separate.","The query is included.","The origin is in the Host header.",""]}],"js45":[{"q":"A path exists but not for this method. What status should you return?","options":["403","404","400","405, with an Allow header listing the methods that do exist"],"answer":3,"why":"Returning 404 hides a client bug that 405 explains immediately.","whyWrong":["That is about permission.","That says the path does not exist.","The request was well-formed.",""]},{"q":"Why must CORS middleware run BEFORE authentication?","options":["It must run after","Authentication needs the CORS headers","A preflight request carries no credentials by design","CORS is faster"],"answer":2,"why":"Otherwise preflights are rejected, the browser never sends the real request, and you debug the wrong thing.","whyWrong":["Before is correct.","It does not.","","Speed is irrelevant."]},{"q":"Silently ignoring an unknown field like `{ role: 'admin' }` in a request body is which vulnerability?","options":["Path traversal","Prototype pollution","Mass assignment","Cross-site scripting"],"answer":2,"why":"Fine until someone spreads the body into a database write. Reject unknown fields rather than ignoring them.","whyWrong":["A file-system problem.","Related, but this is the assignment case.","","A rendering problem."]}],"js46":[{"q":"Why compare an API key with `crypto.timingSafeEqual` rather than `===`?","options":["There is no difference","=== does not work on buffers","=== returns early, leaking through timing how many characters matched","timingSafeEqual is faster"],"answer":2,"why":"Enough attempts and an attacker forges the key one character at a time.","whyWrong":["There is a real one.","It works on strings; the leak is the issue.","","It is slower, deliberately."]},{"q":"A 401 response is non-compliant without which header?","options":["Allow","Cache-Control","WWW-Authenticate","Retry-After"],"answer":2,"why":"It tells the client which scheme to use: Bearer, Basic, and so on.","whyWrong":["That belongs on 405.","Good practice, not required.","","That belongs on 429 and 503."]},{"q":"Where should security headers be set?","options":["In the HTML","Once in middleware, applied to every response","In the reverse proxy only","On each route that needs them"],"answer":1,"why":"A header applied by hand is a header someone forgets on the endpoint that needed it most.","whyWrong":["An API sends no HTML.","","Useful as well, but the app should not depend on it.","That is how one gets missed."]}],"js47":[{"q":"You need to debug a script that finishes in 40 milliseconds. Which flag?","options":["--inspect-brk, which pauses before the first line","kill -USR1","--inspect","--trace-warnings"],"answer":0,"why":"With plain --inspect the process is gone before you can attach.","whyWrong":["","For a process already running.","It runs immediately and exits.","Unrelated."]},{"q":"Why is `node --inspect=0.0.0.0:9229` in a container dangerous?","options":["It disables TLS","It leaks the heap","The inspector is a remote code execution interface","It slows the process down"],"answer":2,"why":"Anyone who can reach that port can evaluate arbitrary code in your process. Bind to localhost and use an SSH tunnel.","whyWrong":["Unrelated.","Only if they attach (which is the point.)","","Overhead is small."]},{"q":"What makes a log line useful during an incident?","options":["Colour coding","Structured JSON with a request id, so one request can be reassembled","A stack trace on every line","A precise timestamp"],"answer":1,"why":"Without the id, one request's story is lost among a thousand interleaved ones.","whyWrong":["Lost in aggregation.","","Noise, and expensive.","Necessary but not sufficient."]}],"js48":[{"q":"Why import from `node:assert/strict` rather than `node:assert`?","options":["The non-strict version compares with == and would say \"5\" equals 5","strict adds more assertion methods","It is faster","There is no difference"],"answer":0,"why":"Loose comparison in a test defeats the purpose of the test.","whyWrong":["","Same methods, different comparison.","Same speed.","A material one."]},{"q":"Which part of testing do people most often skip?","options":["Testing the failures, throws and rejects","Importing assert","Naming the tests","Testing the happy path"],"answer":0,"why":"The failure paths are where the bugs are, and where the behaviour is least obvious.","whyWrong":["","It would not run.","Common, but less costly.","Nobody skips that."]},{"q":"What should you mock?","options":["Your own modules, so tests stay isolated","Every dependency","Nothing","The boundary: the network, the clock, the file system"],"answer":3,"why":"Mocking your own modules couples the test to the structure of the code, which is exactly what you did not want.","whyWrong":["That is the trap.","Over-mocking tests the mocks.","Some boundaries must be controlled.",""]}],"js49":[{"q":"How do you tell normal heap growth from a leak?","options":["heapTotal above 100MB means a leak","rss rising means a leak","A leak is heap that does not come back down after collection, over many cycles","Any growth is a leak"],"answer":2,"why":"Judge from the trend across several minutes, comparing the troughs rather than the peaks.","whyWrong":["Absolute size says nothing.","rss includes much more than the heap.","","The heap expands normally until GC runs."]},{"q":"Reading a CPU profile, what are you looking for?","options":["The largest function","The first frame","A wide frame, width is time spent","A deep stack"],"answer":2,"why":"A deep narrow stack is just a call chain; a wide plateau is where the milliseconds went.","whyWrong":["Size in lines is not cost.","Position is not cost.","","Depth is call nesting."]},{"q":"Which single metric is most worth exporting from a Node service?","options":["Heap size","Event loop lag","Request count","CPU percentage"],"answer":1,"why":"It goes bad before latency does and points at a cause the request logs cannot show.","whyWrong":["Useful, but slower to indicate trouble.","","Says nothing about health.","Ambiguous on its own."]}],"jsdom1":[{"q":"What is the DOM?","options":["The text of your HTML file, kept in memory","The part of the browser that draws pixels","A JavaScript language feature for templates","A live tree of objects the browser builds from your HTML"],"answer":3,"why":"The browser parses your HTML once, builds a tree of objects from it, and from then on the page IS that tree: JavaScript changes the page by changing the tree.","whyWrong":["The HTML text is only the starting recipe; editing it changes nothing after parsing.","Painting happens after the tree changes; the DOM is the model, not the painter.","It is a browser API, not part of the JavaScript language.",""]},{"q":"A user types a comment and your code inserts it into the page. Why is el.textContent the right tool rather than el.innerHTML?","options":["innerHTML only works on div elements","innerHTML parses the string as markup, so user text can become running script","textContent preserves whitespace better","textContent is faster, and speed is the concern"],"answer":1,"why":"innerHTML hands the string to the HTML parser, so \"<img onerror=...>\" in a comment executes. textContent treats every character as literal text. That difference is XSS.","whyWrong":["innerHTML works on any element.","","Whitespace is not the issue, code execution is.","It is faster, but that is not the reason that matters here."]},{"q":"You need to add 200 rows to a list. What is the well-shaped way to do it?","options":["Build the rows in a DocumentFragment, then append it once","Add the rows one per setTimeout so the browser can keep up","Append each row to the document inside the loop","Concatenate one giant HTML string of user data and set innerHTML"],"answer":0,"why":"A fragment is assembled off-page, so the document changes once instead of 200 times, one layout pass, and no string-built markup carrying injection risk.","whyWrong":["","Spreading it over timers just spreads the same cost out.","Each append can trigger layout work; 200 of them is the classic slow loop.","Works until the data contains markup, that is the XSS pattern."]}],"jsdom2":[{"q":"What is event bubbling?","options":["Events are queued until the page finishes loading","Parent handlers always run before child handlers","Events fire twice unless you stop them","After firing on its target, an event travels up through the ancestors"],"answer":3,"why":"The event fires on the element you interacted with, then walks up parent by parent, which is what lets an ancestor hear about it at all.","whyWrong":["Bubbling is about propagation through the tree, not timing.","The target's handler runs first; ancestors hear it on the way up.","It fires once; multiple listeners along the path may hear it.",""]},{"q":"A list gets new rows added at runtime, and clicks on every row must work. Why does one listener on the list (delegation) beat a listener per row?","options":["Because the list listener hears bubbled clicks from ANY row, including rows added later","Because browsers limit how many listeners an element can have","Because per-row listeners are removed automatically when rows change","Because event.target only works on parent elements"],"answer":0,"why":"The parent listener catches clicks bubbling up from whatever rows exist at click time, rows added tomorrow included. event.target says which row it was.","whyWrong":["","There is no such limit; the win is coverage of future rows.","They are not removed automatically, and new rows never got one, which is the bug.","event.target works everywhere; it reports where the event started."]},{"q":"You click a button that sits inside a form, which sits inside a div, all three have click listeners. Who hears it?","options":["Only the button","All three: the button first, then the form, then the div","Only the div, because it is outermost","All three, in whatever order the browser chooses"],"answer":1,"why":"The event fires on the target, then bubbles: button, form, div, inside out, in that order, every time.","whyWrong":["The ancestors hear it too as it bubbles.","","The target hears it first, not the outermost.","The order is defined: target first, then up the tree."]}],"jsdom3":[{"q":"fetch(url) returns a 404. What does the promise do?","options":["Throws synchronously","Rejects, because the request failed","Resolves or rejects depending on the Content-Type","Resolves, a 404 is a completed HTTP conversation"],"answer":3,"why":"fetch rejects only when no HTTP conversation happened at all: DNS failure, no network, CORS block. A 404 is a successful conversation with a negative answer, so it resolves.","whyWrong":["Network work is never synchronous.","The transport succeeded; only the status is bad. That is why res.ok exists.","Content-Type affects parsing, not settlement.",""]},{"q":"Why does well-written fetch code check res.ok before parsing the body?","options":["Because res.ok retries the request when it is false","Because JSON.parse only works when the status is 200","Because an error page parsed as JSON produces a confusing failure far from the cause","Because parsing is slow and should be skipped when possible"],"answer":2,"why":"Without the check, a 500 whose body is an HTML error page hits response.json() and fails with a parse error, pointing at the wrong culprit. Throwing on !res.ok names the real problem at the boundary.","whyWrong":["res.ok is just a boolean; it does nothing on its own.","Parsing does not consult the status, that is exactly the problem.","","Cost is not the point; correctness of the error is."]},{"q":"The console shows a CORS error calling an API from your page. Where does the fix live?","options":["In the browser settings of your users","In HTML, use a form instead of fetch","On the API's server, it must state that your origin is allowed","In your JavaScript (add the right request headers)"],"answer":2,"why":"CORS is the browser refusing to let your page read a cross-origin response unless the SERVER grants it. No client-side code can grant permission the server did not give.","whyWrong":["Users cannot and should not fix it.","Dodging fetch dodges the protection, not the problem.","","Request headers cannot authorize your origin; only response headers can."]}],"jsdom4":[{"q":"What does new FormData(formElement) give you?","options":["The form's HTML as a string","Key–value pairs of every named field's current value","A JSON string ready to POST","A validated copy of the form"],"answer":1,"why":"FormData reads every named control's current value in one line, the standard first step of a JavaScript-handled submit.","whyWrong":["It reads values, not markup.","","It is not JSON; convert with Object.fromEntries(fd) if you need that.","It reads; validation is a separate, deliberate step."]},{"q":"The browser's built-in validation (required, type=\"email\") passes. Why must the server still validate the same data?","options":["Because browser validation only runs in Chrome","Because HTML attributes expire after the page loads","It does not need to, client validation is sufficient","Because anyone can send a request to your server without ever loading your form"],"answer":3,"why":"The form is advice to honest browsers. curl, scripts and attackers talk to your server directly, so the server-side check is the only one that is actually enforced.","whyWrong":["It runs in every modern browser (that is not the gap.)","Attributes do not expire; they just do not bind anyone.","Client-only validation is the classic vulnerability.",""]},{"q":"In a JavaScript-handled form, what does event.preventDefault() in the submit handler do?","options":["Disables the submit button until validation passes","Clears the form after submission","Stops the browser's own navigate-and-reload submission so your code can take over","Blocks invalid values from entering the fields"],"answer":2,"why":"The browser's default on submit is to serialize the form and navigate away. preventDefault cancels that navigation, leaving your fetch in charge of what happens next.","whyWrong":["Button state is your code's job.","Nothing is cleared; the default action is simply cancelled.","","It does not touch field values."]}],"jsdate":[{"q":"What is a JavaScript Date, underneath?","options":["A timezone-aware timestamp that remembers where it was created","A number of milliseconds since Jan 1 1970 UTC, with methods on it","A calendar object storing year, month and day fields","A string in ISO format"],"answer":1,"why":"A Date is just that one number. It has no timezone inside it, the timezone appears only when you FORMAT it for a human.","whyWrong":["It remembers no zone; toLocaleString applies the viewer's zone at display time.","","The fields are computed from the number on demand.","Strings are one way in and out, not the representation."]},{"q":"A user in New York saves their birthday as new Date(\"2026-03-03\") and the app later shows March 2nd. Why?","options":["The Date object expired","Daylight saving time removed a day","getMonth is zero-based, shifting the date","A date-only string parses as midnight UTC, which is the previous evening in New York"],"answer":3,"why":"\"2026-03-03\" means 2026-03-03T00:00:00Z. Midnight UTC is 7pm on March 2nd in New York, so formatting in local time shows the previous day. Store instants in full ISO form with the Z.","whyWrong":["Dates do not expire.","DST shifts hours, not the parsing rule.","getMonth is zero-based but that affects reading months, not this.",""]},{"q":"What does new Date(\"garbage\") do?","options":["Returns null","Returns an Invalid Date whose every method yields NaN, silently","Returns the current time as a fallback","Throws a TypeError immediately"],"answer":1,"why":"No throw, no null, an Invalid Date object, and every calculation touching it becomes NaN and spreads. Guard with Number.isNaN(d.getTime()) at the boundary.","whyWrong":["It is a Date object, just a poisoned one.","","There is no fallback to now.","It never throws for bad strings (that is the trap.)"]}],"jsfp":[{"q":"Which property makes a function pure?","options":["Same arguments always give the same result, and nothing outside it changes","It is shorter than ten lines","It uses const instead of let","It is declared with an arrow"],"answer":0,"why":"Purity is two promises: deterministic output, and no side effects, no mutated arguments, no shared state, no I/O. That is what makes it testable with a call and an assertion.","whyWrong":["","Length is style, not purity.","const inside does not stop mutation of the outside.","Syntax has nothing to do with it."]},{"q":"items.sort(byDate) versus items.toSorted(byDate), what is the actual difference?","options":["sort only works on numbers","There is none; toSorted is an alias","sort reorders the caller's array in place; toSorted returns a sorted copy","toSorted is faster"],"answer":2,"why":"sort mutates, every other holder of that array sees the new order, a surprise at a distance. toSorted leaves the original untouched, which is the immutability discipline in one method choice.","whyWrong":["sort handles anything with a comparator.","One mutates, one copies; that is the whole story.","","Copying is if anything slightly more work (the point is safety.)"]},{"q":"const copy = { ...user }, what does this copy, exactly?","options":["Only string properties","The top level only; nested objects are still shared with the original","Nothing, it creates an alias to the same object","Everything, at every depth"],"answer":1,"why":"Spread copies one level. copy.name is independent, but copy.address points at the SAME address object, so the discipline is per-level: copy what you change, at the level you change it.","whyWrong":["All own enumerable properties come along, whatever their type.","","It is a real new object; only the nested values are shared.","Deep copying needs structuredClone."]}],"jsgen":[{"q":"What happens when you CALL a generator function, const g = steps()?","options":["The body runs to the first yield","It starts running in a background thread","Nothing runs; you get a paused machine that advances on next()","The body runs to completion and returns an array"],"answer":2,"why":"Calling a generator executes none of its body. Each next() runs to the following yield and pauses, with all local state preserved between calls.","whyWrong":["Not even to the first yield, that takes the first next().","There are no threads here; it is a pausable function.","","It never runs eagerly to completion."]},{"q":"Why is function* naturals() { let n = 1; while (true) yield n++; } not an infinite loop bug?","options":["Generators are lazy: the body only advances when a consumer asks for the next value","yield inside while(true) throws","It is a bug; this code hangs","The engine caps generators at 10,000 values"],"answer":0,"why":"Nothing runs until next() is called, and each call runs exactly to the next yield. An endless sequence is fine as long as the consumer stops asking.","whyWrong":["","yield in a loop is the normal pattern.","It hangs only if a consumer demands values forever, spread would, take(5) would not.","There is no cap."]},{"q":"A for...of loop over a generator holding an open resource exits early with break. What happens to the generator's try/finally?","options":["break calls the generator's return(), and the finally runs","The generator keeps running in the background","The finally runs only if the generator was fully consumed","The finally is skipped (cleanup needs a manual close call)"],"answer":0,"why":"Exiting a for...of early, break, throw or return, calls the iterator's return(), which resumes the generator as if a return happened at the paused yield. The finally runs, so cleanup is guaranteed.","whyWrong":["","Nothing runs in the background; the generator finishes right then.","Early abandonment triggers it too, that is the guarantee.","That is why the protocol includes return() (no manual close needed.)"]}],"jsemit":[{"q":"bus.emit(\"order\", 42), when do the listeners run?","options":["In parallel on worker threads","Synchronously, they have all run before emit returns","On the next microtask, like a promise","On the next timer tick"],"answer":1,"why":"emit calls every listener for the event, in registration order, right now. The line after emit runs after all listeners are done.","whyWrong":["One thread, one loop, listeners are just function calls.","","Promises defer; emitters do not.","Timers are macrotasks; this is a plain function call underneath."]},{"q":"An EventEmitter emits \"error\" and nobody registered an error listener. What does Node do?","options":["Throws, and an uncaught one crashes the process","Retries the operation that failed","Silently drops it","Logs a warning and continues"],"answer":0,"why":"An error nobody is listening for should not vanish, so Node throws it. Every long-lived emitter, streams, servers, sockets, needs an error listener.","whyWrong":["","Node knows nothing about the operation; it just refuses to swallow the error.","Deliberately not (silent errors are worse than crashes.)","It is a throw, not a warning."]},{"q":"A request handler does bus.on(\"tick\", () => ...) on a module-level emitter. Why is this the classic leak?","options":["Emitters can only hold ten listeners","The closure prevents the emitter from being garbage collected","Each request adds a listener that is never removed, and the emitter keeps them all alive","on() is slower than once()"],"answer":2,"why":"Every request adds one more listener; none are removed. The emitter references each listener, each closure references what it captured, and none of it can be collected while subscribed. Subscribing is allocating.","whyWrong":["Ten is just the warning threshold, not a limit, the warning exists to surface exactly this bug.","It is the reverse: the long-lived emitter keeps the listeners alive.","","Speed is irrelevant here."]}],"jssec":[{"q":"What is the one cure that fixes SQL injection, XSS and command injection alike?","options":["Escaping quotes in user input","Rejecting input containing special characters","Keeping user data out of the executable channel: parameters, textContent, argument arrays","Base64-encoding everything before use"],"answer":2,"why":"Every injection is user text concatenated into something that gets executed. Parameterised queries, textContent and execFile argument arrays all make the same move: data travels as data, never as code.","whyWrong":["Escaping is a fragile, host-specific patch on the wrong design.","Legitimate input contains special characters; blocklists always leak.","","Encoding without separating channels changes nothing."]},{"q":"An attacker sends {\"__proto__\": {\"isAdmin\": true}} to an endpoint that deep-merges it into a config object. What can happen?","options":["A syntax error rejects the request","Object.prototype gains isAdmin, so EVERY object in the process reports isAdmin as true","Only the config object gets an extra property","Nothing: JSON cannot carry a key named __proto__"],"answer":1,"why":"A naive merge that walks into a __proto__ key writes onto Object.prototype itself, poisoning every object in the program. Refuse the keys __proto__, constructor and prototype in merged input.","whyWrong":["It is valid JSON; nothing rejects it for you.","","That is what makes pollution worse than a normal bad write, the damage is global.","JSON.parse happily creates it as an own property; the danger is what your merge does next."]},{"q":"A pull request's only change to package-lock.json is a surprise you cannot explain. Why treat it seriously?","options":["The lockfile pins the exact code you ship; a changed pin can swap in different code, and installing runs it","It is harmless, since npm regenerates the file from the manifest on the next install anyway","Lockfile changes always break the build, so a diff touching only the lockfile should be rejected","Lockfiles are generated artifacts and cannot meaningfully be reviewed by a human reader"],"answer":0,"why":"The lockfile is a security file: it records the exact bytes npm ci will install, and install scripts execute on your machines. An unexplained pin change deserves the suspicion you would give a binary blob.","whyWrong":["","npm ci installs exactly what it says, that is why what it says matters.","Often it builds fine, that is what makes it easy to wave through.","It is JSON; diffs are reviewable, which is the point."]}],"jsmem":[{"q":"When does the garbage collector free an object?","options":["Immediately when the function that created it returns","When it becomes unreachable from the roots, globals, the stack, live closures","When memory pressure forces the OS to intervene","When you set every variable that held it to null"],"answer":1,"why":"One rule: alive means findable by following references from a root. Unreachable means garbage, collected whenever the engine pleases.","whyWrong":["Only if nothing escaped the call, a returned closure keeps the scope alive.","","Collection is the engine's routine work, not an OS emergency.","Nulling variables is only one way to sever paths, any unreachability counts, and one surviving path keeps it alive."]},{"q":"Two objects reference each other in a cycle, and nothing else references either. Are they leaked?","options":["Only if they contain closures, since a closure holds its scope and defeats the collector's analysis","Yes, each keeps the other alive, so a reference cycle can never be collected once created","Yes, until the page reloads, at which point the entire heap is discarded along with the cycle","No, reachability is measured from the roots, and no root reaches them; a cycle of garbage is still garbage"],"answer":3,"why":"Mark-and-sweep starts at the roots. The cycle's internal references are irrelevant if no root leads in, this exact case is why reference counting lost.","whyWrong":["Closures change nothing about the rule.","That is how reference COUNTING would fail; engines do not count.","No reload needed.",""]},{"q":"When is a WeakMap the right container?","options":["For primitive keys like strings and numbers","Whenever you want faster lookups than Map","For attaching data TO objects without keeping those objects alive","For storing lists you iterate often"],"answer":2,"why":"WeakMap keys are held weakly: when the last real reference to the key object drops, the entry evaporates with it, a cache that cannot leak its keys.","whyWrong":["Keys must be objects; a primitive cannot be collected out from under you.","Speed is comparable; lifetime is the difference.","","WeakMaps are deliberately not iterable, you cannot list keys the collector may be removing."]}],"jswork":[{"q":"What is a worker, in one sentence?","options":["A promise that runs on the GPU","A separate thread with its own event loop, communicating only by message passing","A faster event loop for your main thread","A background tab"],"answer":1,"why":"Workers add more event loops rather than speeding up yours, separate thread, separate globals, no shared variables, messages as the only channel.","whyWrong":["No GPU, no promises, a thread.","","Your loop stays exactly as fast; the work moves elsewhere.","Same page, different thread."]},{"q":"An endpoint spends 2 seconds waiting on a database query. Should that move to a worker?","options":["Only if there are more than ten concurrent requests","Yes (two seconds would block the event loop)","No, awaited I/O does not occupy the loop; a worker adds copying and solves nothing","Yes, but only with SharedArrayBuffer"],"answer":2,"why":"While a query is in flight your code is not running, the loop is free for other requests. Workers exist for CPU-bound work, where your code IS the thing occupying the loop.","whyWrong":["Concurrency is what the loop already does well for I/O.","Waiting is not computing; the loop handles thousands of concurrent waits.","","Shared memory is for a different problem entirely."]},{"q":"You postMessage a class instance to a worker. What arrives?","options":["A DataCloneError, objects cannot cross","A live reference, changes on either side are visible to both","A plain object with the instance's own data properties, methods and prototype gone","The instance, methods and all"],"answer":2,"why":"Structured clone copies data: own properties survive, the prototype link and methods do not. Functions and DOM nodes refuse to clone at all. Send data, reconstruct behaviour on the other side.","whyWrong":["Plain data clones fine; it is functions and DOM nodes that throw.","Messages are deep copies, no sharing, which is why there are no data races.","","Methods live on the prototype, which does not travel."]}]};
